[
  {
    "title": "Esotericism Was the Rule",
    "body": "Kelley traces the book back to George Sarantitus, whose own volume runs roughly 500 pages, far more than the documentary could carry, and to Arthur Melzer's Philosophy Between the Lines, which gathers thousands of quotations from antiquity through the modern age to argue that writing on multiple levels was the rule rather than the exception. Kelley says the idea had vanished from discourse for a couple of hundred years until Leo Strauss revived it at the University of Chicago, and he cites Francis Bacon on the two manners of writing, the enigmatical and the disclosed, the first meant to remove the vulgar from the secrets of knowledge."
  },
  {
    "title": "Three Reasons to Conceal",
    "body": "Socrates, the philosopher of the Athenian streets, wrote nothing and was tried and put to death around 399 BC on three charges, corrupting the youth, impiety, and introducing new gods to the city state. Kelley argues that Plato answered his teacher's objections to writing by working on layered levels of meaning, and he separates three motives: defensive esotericism, shielding a writer from persecution, whether in Athens, Nazi Germany, or the Stalinist Soviet Union; pedagogical esotericism, which he likens to a calculus teacher assigning problems whose answers he already knows; and protective esotericism, guarding a traditional society from truths thought to be dangerous."
  },
  {
    "title": "Tradition Against the Blank Page",
    "body": "Every ancient culture, Kelley notes, from Sparta to Persia to an Egypt whose Old Kingdom artwork looks nearly unchanged a thousand years later, held an entrenched identity because survival demanded solidarity. He quotes a line from Rome's Last Citizen that the myth of cultural decline sat as firmly in the ancient mind as the myth of progress sits in ours, then sets against it the purely rational alternative built on a blank sheet of paper: the caste divisions and removal of children in the Republic, the Chinese Cultural Revolution later begging Taiwan for the traditions it destroyed, and Napoleon's attempt at a ten day week."
  },
  {
    "title": "Two Perfect Societies Annihilate",
    "body": "The Atlantis story survives in two dialogues, Timaeus, which is long and complete, and Critias, which breaks off at roughly a quarter of that length. Critias credits it to Solon's visit to Egypt around 593 BC, where priests described a war 9,000 years earlier, dated in the book near 9,600 BC, between an aggressive Atlantis and a protothenian Athens whose rational city state Plato explicitly equates with the society Socrates had just outlined. Kelley emphasizes that both armies are swallowed by earthquake and flood, that Socrates asks to see his city put into motion, and that the promised third dialogue by Hermocrates was never written."
  },
  {
    "title": "Vinegar Iron and the Republic",
    "body": "Kelley and Garton read the mutual destruction as the eternal quarrel between theory and practice, the contemplative life against the life of action, with Aristotle's poiesis offered as a possible mercurial bridge and eudaimonia as thriving inside a functioning city state. Philosophy and the traditional polis, Kelley maintains, corrode each other the way vinegar and iron do. He points to the mixed constitution discussed in Plato's Statesman, taken up by Polybius and named at least twice in the Federalist Papers, and warns that the Roman version of that experiment ended when Julius Caesar crossed the Rubicon."
  }
]

